<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.2.4: Making a Perfect Square</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02357</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.2.4: Making a Perfect Square</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c2834b44-2284-4795-b01a-7dce24c66b81</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><para id="para-00001">For questions 1 – 3, what could be added to each expression to make it a perfect square?</para>
<section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002"><title>Cool Down Activity</title><exercise id="_543b9b67-dd3b-4a76-ade2-553feaddce4c" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00001"><para id="para-00002">Enter what could be added to make the expression a perfect square.</para><para id="para-00003">\(x^2+12x\)</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00001"><para id="para-00004">Correct! The constant term \(c=36\) can be added to make the perfect square \(x^2+12x+36\).</para><para id="para-00005">The correct answer is: The constant term \(c=36\) can be added to make the perfect square \(x^2+12x+36\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="b90d8ac3-5961-4630-8cd0-4f4dca82c72c" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00002"><para id="para-00006">Enter what could be added to make the expression a perfect square.</para><para id="para-00007">\(x^2−6x+1\)</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00002"><para id="para-00008">Correct! 8 can be added to the constant term \(c=1\) to make the perfect square \(x^2-6x+9\).</para><para id="para-00009">The correct answer is: 8 can be added to the constant term \(c=1\) to make the perfect square \(x^2-6x+9\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_5eeca6f7-4863-4592-ae57-aafccf5fa58c" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00003"><para id="para-00010">Enter what could be added to make the expression a perfect square.</para><para id="para-00011">\(x^2+14x−10\)</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00003"><para id="para-00012">Correct! 59 can be added to the constant term \(c=-10\) to make the perfect square \(x^2+14x+49\).</para><para id="para-00013">The correct answer is: 59 can be added to the constant term \(c=-10\) to make the perfect square \(x^2+14x+49\).</para></solution></exercise></section><example id="exam-00001"><exercise>
  <problem id="prob-00004">
    
    <para id="para-00014">Solve the equation \(x^2−16x=-60\) by <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">completing the square</term>. Show your reasoning.</para>
    <para id="para-00015">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00004">
  <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00017">\(\begin{aligned} x^{2}-16 x &amp;=-60 \\ x^{2}-16 x+64 &amp;=-60+64 \\ x^{2}-16 x+64 &amp;=4 \\ (x-8)^{2} &amp;=4 \\ \sqrt{(x-8)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{4} \\ x-8 &amp;=\pm 2 \\ x-8 &amp;=2 \\ x-8 &amp;=-2 \\ \\ x=6 &amp; \text { or } x=10 \end{aligned}\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
